{
 "schema": 4,
 "question": "B. Is this measure already running in East Uusimaa wellbeing services county (or in the HUS university hospital area that treats its patients)?\nA. Does recent evidence support the size of effect we assume: does this measure raise the share of lung cancer patients who get curative-intent treatment, and by how much?\nMeasure: Fitness assessment and prehabilitation before deciding against curative treatment. Before a stage I-III patient is judged too frail, they get an exercise test, a geriatric assessment if over 75, and four weeks of supervised exercise, breathing training and help to stop smoking. Then the decision is made again.",
 "area_id": "01",
 "verdict": "mixed",
 "effect_check": "weaker",
 "effect_summary_en": "",
 "supported_en": "A 2022 Cochrane review (10 randomised trials, 636 patients) found that exercise before surgery cut lung complications after surgery (risk ratio 0.45) and shortened the hospital stay by about 2.2 days.",
 "not_supported_en": "No page I read measures how many more patients receive curative-intent treatment after prehabilitation, so the assumed 0.5-point gain is not shown.",
 "in_use_here": "no_evidence",
 "in_use_scope": "none",
 "in_use_quote": null,
 "in_use_source": null,
 "in_use_summary_en": "",
 "missing_en": "",
 "summary_en": "The pages I read show no public sign of this measure in East Uusimaa or HUS. HUS's lung cancer care-pathway page says surgery is the first choice unless physical condition, other illnesses or lung function prevent it. HUS's Cancer Centre offers physiotherapy only when a doctor refers the patient, and no page describes a structured programme to build fitness before a treatment decision. For effect, a 2022 Cochrane review found that exercise before surgery cut lung complications after surgery (risk ratio 0.45). It did not measure any gain in the share of patients treated with curative intent.",
 "unanswered_en": "I found no HUS, Porvoo Hospital or East Uusimaa page on exercise testing, geriatric assessment or prehabilitation before fitness decisions in lung cancer, and no study measuring a gain in curative-treatment share.",
